d2d1: report the layer-mask outcome, not the rect decomposition's opinion

The mask diagnostics were lying, and lying in the direction that costs time.

install_mask_rects() logs "bounding box in force" whenever it declines to
decompose a mask into scissor rects. But declining is not the outcome:
PushLayer then tries the path stencil, which since the histogram work carries
almost all of those masks exactly. A 2026-08-13 Premiere capture holds 668
FILLMODE=ALTERNATE lines and 436 non-rectangle lines, every one of them
reading as a live correctness hole -- and the wiki's standing P1 theory for
the vanishing tool buttons rested on exactly those lines.

Measured with a new harness (preserved-fixes/harnesses/d2d-alternate-hole-probe.c)
on 11.10-61, three ALTERNATE geometry groups used as layer masks:

  n=2 rect ring    -> mask_rects decomposes into 4 scissors   hole preserved
  n=3, two holes   -> declines, logs "bounding box in force"  hole preserved (stencil)
  diamond ring     -> declines, logs "bounding box in force"  hole preserved (stencil)

So the ALTERNATE hole is already closed. Only the message was wrong.

The reason is now recorded on the context and reported by PushLayer once the
stencil decision is known:

  stencilled     -> MASKSTENCIL <reason> ... the bounding box is NOT in force
                    (optrace-gated; this is the normal, correct case)
  not stencilled -> MASKFALLBACK <reason> ... BOUNDING BOX IN FORCE
                    (unconditional for the first 16, then counted -- this one
                     is the real defect and must not need a debug flag to see)

The single-shape case (a mask that is not a geometry group at all -- Lightroom's
histogram curves) was silent in both directions before and is now reported the
same way.

Both PushLayer entry points carried a verbatim copy of the stencil block; they
are one helper now. Wiring one and forgetting the other only shows up in
whichever app happens to use the other vtbl.

Adds NEUTRON_D2D_STENCIL=0. Operationally it answers "is the stencil doing
this?" on a user's box without a rebuild. Methodologically it is the only way
to exercise the BOUNDING-BOX-IN-FORCE branch: with the stencil on, every mask
the probe can build is carried, so that branch would have shipped never having
run once. With it off the probe floods 3/3 and the branch fires on exactly the
two cases whose pixels actually flood -- message and pixels agree.

Verified against d2d-layermask-probe, d2d-greenfill-selftest, d2d-earclip-probe,
d2d-earclip-strict and d2d-gradient-probe: no change.

Also fixes a double count found next door: the ID2D1DeviceContext PushLayer
called neutron_op(NEUTRON_OP_PUSHLAYER) twice, so the op census reported
double the PushLayers for any app on that vtbl while the ID2D1RenderTarget
entry point counted once. That census is the ops-per-present table that
cracked the WIC retained-content bug.

--- a/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/d2d1_private.h
@@ -219,6 +219,23 @@
 
 /* NEUTRON LAYER-MASK RECTS (env NEUTRON_D2D_MASKRECTS, default ON, 0 to disable): scissor
  * draws inside a layer to the mask's individual rectangles rather than their bounding box. */
+/* NEUTRON: kill switch for the path-mask stencil (NEUTRON_D2D_STENCIL=0 -> off).
+ * Two reasons it exists. Operationally, the stencil is the newest and most invasive thing in the
+ * layer path, and turning it off without a rebuild is the fastest way to answer "is the stencil
+ * doing this?" on a user's box. Methodologically, it is the ONLY way to exercise the
+ * bounding-box-in-force branch of the mask report: with the stencil on, every mask the probe can
+ * construct is carried, so that branch would ship having never once run. */
+static inline BOOL neutron_stencil(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0)
+    {
+        char buf[8];
+        v = (GetEnvironmentVariableA("NEUTRON_D2D_STENCIL", buf, sizeof(buf)) && buf[0] == '0') ? 0 : 1;
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
 static inline BOOL neutron_maskrects(void)
 {
     static int v = -1;
@@ -715,6 +732,14 @@
     D2D1_RECT_F mask_rects[256];
     unsigned mask_rect_count;
     unsigned mask_owner;        /* layer index that installed the list, +1 (0 = none) */
+    /* NEUTRON: why the rect decomposition declined this layer's mask, recorded rather than
+     * logged. The old code logged "bounding box in force" AT the decline -- but the decline is
+     * not the outcome: PushLayer then tries the path stencil, which usually carries the mask
+     * exactly. A 2026-08-13 Premiere capture is full of those lines (668 ALTERNATE, 436
+     * non-rectangle) and every one of them reads as a live correctness hole that isn't. Hold the
+     * reason until the stencil decision is known, then report what ACTUALLY happened. */
+    const char *mask_declined;  /* static string, NULL = the mask was representable */
+    unsigned mask_declined_n;   /* member count that went with it */
     unsigned last_op;           /* NEUTRON op-trace: most recent primitive id */
     /* NEUTRON REGION READBACK: a persistent staging texture for pulling just the drawn
      * region back to the CPU, instead of IDXGISurface1::GetDC copying the WHOLE surface
--- a/dlls/d2d1/device.c
+++ b/dlls/d2d1/device.c
@@ -403,6 +403,15 @@
     return count;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: record WHY the rect decomposition declined a mask. Deliberately not logged here --
+ * see mask_declined in d2d1_private.h. The reason must be a string literal; it outlives the call. */
+static void d2d_device_context_mask_declined(struct d2d_device_context *context,
+        const char *reason, unsigned n)
+{
+    context->mask_declined = reason;
+    context->mask_declined_n = n;
+}
+
 /* NEUTRON LAYER-MASK RECTS: if a layer's geometric mask is a WINDING geometry group of
  * disjoint rectangles -- a dirty region -- record the pieces in device pixels so each draw
  * inside the layer can be scissored to them individually. Clipping to the bounding box
@@ -424,9 +433,16 @@
     BOOL ok = TRUE;
 
     if (!neutron_maskrects() || context->mask_owner)
-        return FALSE;
+        return FALSE;   /* not a decline: an ancestor layer already installed the rect list */
     if (FAILED(ID2D1Geometry_QueryInterface(mask, &IID_ID2D1GeometryGroup, (void **)&group)))
+    {
+        /* A single shape rather than a group -- Lightroom's histogram curves are these. There is
+         * nothing to decompose, so the stencil is the ONLY thing standing between the mask and a
+         * plain bounding-box clip. Record it so the report below can say which of those two
+         * actually happened; before, this path was silent either way. */
+        d2d_device_context_mask_declined(context, "mask is a single shape, not a geometry group", 1);
         return FALSE;
+    }
 
     n = ID2D1GeometryGroup_GetSourceGeometryCount(group);
     fillmode = ID2D1GeometryGroup_GetFillMode(group);
@@ -453,18 +469,15 @@
          * (one occurrence in a 76-minute editing capture), so it cannot spam a log. Everything
          * else in this block stays behind NEUTRON_D2D_OPTRACE, because the non-rectangle case
          * fires thousands of times per session. */
-        if (n > ARRAY_SIZE(srcs))
-            ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK OVER-CAP n=%u (limit %u) -- bounding box in force; "
-                "raise mask_rects[] in d2d1_private.h\n", n, (unsigned)ARRAY_SIZE(srcs));
+        d2d_device_context_mask_declined(context,
+                n > ARRAY_SIZE(srcs) ? "OVER-CAP (raise mask_rects[] in d2d1_private.h)"
+                                     : "FILLMODE=ALTERNATE (a hole; the bbox would over-include it)",
+                n);
 
         if (neutron_optrace())
         {
             static LONG dumps;
 
-            if (n <= ARRAY_SIZE(srcs))
-                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK FILLMODE=ALTERNATE n=%u -- bounding box in force "
-                    "(a hole; the bbox over-includes it)\n", n);
-
             if (n && n <= ARRAY_SIZE(srcs) && InterlockedIncrement(&dumps) <= 8)
             {
                 ID2D1Geometry *dump[ARRAY_SIZE(context->mask_rects)] = {0};
@@ -586,8 +599,7 @@
     }
     if (!ok || !count)
     {
-        if (neutron_optrace())
-            ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK non-rectangle member (n=%u) -- bounding box in force\n", n);
+        d2d_device_context_mask_declined(context, "non-rectangle member", n);
         return FALSE;
     }
 
@@ -600,9 +612,7 @@
 
         if (count != 2 || !(fc = d2d_mask_rects_from_alternate_pair(&rects[0], &rects[1], frame)))
         {
-            if (neutron_optrace())
-                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK ALTERNATE pair not a hole (count=%u) -- bounding box in force\n",
-                        count);
+            d2d_device_context_mask_declined(context, "ALTERNATE pair is not a hole", count);
             return FALSE;
         }
         if (neutron_optrace())
@@ -620,8 +630,7 @@
             if (rects[i].left < rects[j].right && rects[j].left < rects[i].right
                     && rects[i].top < rects[j].bottom && rects[j].top < rects[i].bottom)
             {
-                if (neutron_optrace())
-                    ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK overlapping pieces (n=%u) -- bounding box in force\n", count);
+                d2d_device_context_mask_declined(context, "overlapping pieces", count);
                 return FALSE;
             }
         }
@@ -3327,29 +3336,24 @@
     context->drawing_state.transform = saved;
 }
 
-static void STDMETHODCALLTYPE d2d_device_context_PushLayer(ID2D1DeviceContext6 *iface,
-        const D2D1_LAYER_PARAMETERS *layer_parameters, ID2D1Layer *layer)
+/* NEUTRON PATH-MASK STENCIL: the layer clip is the mask's BOUNDING BOX, correct only when the
+ * mask is that rectangle or was decomposed into mask_rects. When it is a genuine shape --
+ * Lightroom masks each histogram channel to its curve, then fills the whole box -- the box lets
+ * the fill flood it. Stencil the shape as well and test every draw inside the layer against it.
+ * The box clip stays: a valid superset, and the cheap scissor still does most of the rejection.
+ *
+ * Both ID2D1DeviceContext::PushLayer and the older ID2D1RenderTarget::PushLayer land here. They
+ * used to carry a verbatim copy of this each; wiring one and forgetting the other is a bug that
+ * only shows up in whichever app happens to use the other vtbl. One copy now. */
+static void d2d_device_context_push_layer_stencil(struct d2d_device_context *context,
+        const D2D1_LAYER_PARAMETERS *layer_parameters)
 {
-    NEUTRON_API_TIME();
-    struct d2d_device_context *context = impl_from_ID2D1DeviceContext(iface);
-    neutron_op(context, NEUTRON_OP_PUSHLAYER);
+    BOOL stencilled = FALSE;
 
-    TRACE("iface %p, layer_parameters %p, layer %p.\n", iface, layer_parameters, layer);
-
-    neutron_op(context, NEUTRON_OP_PUSHLAYER);
-    if (layer_parameters)
-        d2d_device_context_push_layer_clip(context, &layer_parameters->contentBounds,
-                layer_parameters->geometricMask, &layer_parameters->maskTransform);
-
-    /* NEUTRON PATH-MASK STENCIL: the clip above is the mask's BOUNDING BOX, correct only when the
-     * mask is that rectangle or decomposed into mask_rects. When it is a genuine shape --
-     * Lightroom masks each histogram channel to its curve, then fills the whole box -- the box
-     * lets the fill flood it. Stencil the shape as well and test every draw inside the layer
-     * against it. The box clip stays: a valid superset, and the cheap scissor still does most of
-     * the rejection. */
     if (context->layer_depth < ARRAY_SIZE(context->layer_stencilled))
         context->layer_stencilled[context->layer_depth] = 0;
-    if (layer_parameters && layer_parameters->geometricMask && !context->mask_rect_count
+    if (neutron_stencil()
+            && layer_parameters && layer_parameters->geometricMask && !context->mask_rect_count
             && context->layer_depth < ARRAY_SIZE(context->stencil_mask)
             && context->target.type == D2D_TARGET_BITMAP
             && d2d_device_context_mask_is_stencillable(layer_parameters->geometricMask)
@@ -3373,7 +3377,62 @@
         context->stencil_mask_transform[slot] = layer_parameters->maskTransform;
         context->layer_stencilled[slot] = 1;
         ++context->stencil_depth;
+        stencilled = TRUE;
+    }
+
+    /* Now -- and only now -- is the outcome known. Report what happened, not what the rect
+     * decomposition wanted. Declining to decompose is NOT a defect if the stencil then carries
+     * the mask exactly; saying "bounding box in force" there sent a reader hunting a closed bug.
+     * The genuinely defective case is the other branch: no rects AND no stencil means the layer
+     * really is clipped to the mask's bounding box, so a fill inside it can wipe content the
+     * mask excludes. That one is worth saying out loud, but it is also potentially per-frame, so
+     * cap it and count the rest. */
+    if (context->mask_declined)
+    {
+        if (stencilled)
+        {
+            if (neutron_optrace())
+                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKSTENCIL %s (n=%u) -- no rect decomposition, but the path "
+                    "stencil carries this mask; the bounding box is NOT in force\n",
+                    context->mask_declined, context->mask_declined_n);
+        }
+        else
+        {
+            static LONG shouted, suppressed;
+
+            if (InterlockedIncrement(&shouted) <= 16)
+                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK %s (n=%u) -- BOUNDING BOX IN FORCE (no rects, no "
+                    "stencil): a fill inside this layer can wipe content the mask excludes\n",
+                    context->mask_declined, context->mask_declined_n);
+            else if (InterlockedIncrement(&suppressed) == 1)
+                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK further occurrences suppressed -- "
+                    "set NEUTRON_D2D_OPTRACE=1 for all of them\n");
+            else if (neutron_optrace())
+                ERR("neutron-dr: MASKFALLBACK %s (n=%u) -- BOUNDING BOX IN FORCE\n",
+                    context->mask_declined, context->mask_declined_n);
+        }
+        context->mask_declined = NULL;
     }
+}
+
+static void STDMETHODCALLTYPE d2d_device_context_PushLayer(ID2D1DeviceContext6 *iface,
+        const D2D1_LAYER_PARAMETERS *layer_parameters, ID2D1Layer *layer)
+{
+    NEUTRON_API_TIME();
+    struct d2d_device_context *context = impl_from_ID2D1DeviceContext(iface);
+
+    TRACE("iface %p, layer_parameters %p, layer %p.\n", iface, layer_parameters, layer);
+
+    /* NEUTRON: counted ONCE. This was counted twice here and once in the ID2D1RenderTarget
+     * PushLayer below, so the op census reported double the PushLayers for any app on the modern
+     * vtbl and the two entry points disagreed. That census is the table that cracked the WIC
+     * retained-content bug -- an inflated count in it misleads exactly the same way. */
+    neutron_op(context, NEUTRON_OP_PUSHLAYER);
+    if (layer_parameters)
+        d2d_device_context_push_layer_clip(context, &layer_parameters->contentBounds,
+                layer_parameters->geometricMask, &layer_parameters->maskTransform);
+
+    d2d_device_context_push_layer_stencil(context, layer_parameters);
     ++context->layer_depth;
 
     if (context->target.type == D2D_TARGET_COMMAND_LIST)
@@ -4592,39 +4651,7 @@
         d2d_device_context_push_layer_clip(context, &layer_parameters->contentBounds,
                 layer_parameters->geometricMask, &layer_parameters->maskTransform);
 
-    /* NEUTRON PATH-MASK STENCIL: the clip above is the mask's BOUNDING BOX, correct only when the
-     * mask is that rectangle or decomposed into mask_rects. When it is a genuine shape --
-     * Lightroom masks each histogram channel to its curve, then fills the whole box -- the box
-     * lets the fill flood it. Stencil the shape as well and test every draw inside the layer
-     * against it. The box clip stays: a valid superset, and the cheap scissor still does most of
-     * the rejection. */
-    if (context->layer_depth < ARRAY_SIZE(context->layer_stencilled))
-        context->layer_stencilled[context->layer_depth] = 0;
